{
 "format": "wtc-reading/2 (whole piece)",
 "README": [
  "A whole-piece reading: every mark an edition prints for one fugue, in every voice and bar, attached to the game's own notes.",
  "Notes are addressed as in data/touch/pieces.json: v = voice index (here 0 Soprano, 1 Alto, 2 Bass, pieces.json voiceNames), on = onset tick (tpq 1920; a 4/4 bar is 7680 ticks; bar n starts at (n-1)*7680). p (MIDI) and name are a check, bar and pos (sixteenths from the barline) are for people.",
  "marks[]: one note's articulation. art is one of staccato (dot), wedge (Keil: shorter and sharper), tenuto (dash: full value, a slight lean), portato (dash over dot: held a little apart, each note weighted), accent (>), marcato (^ over a dot: struck hard and left), fermata. A note may have two marks (wedge+tenuto).",
  "slurs[]: spans in one voice, from and to both included. kind \"slur\" = a group played as one gesture (joined inside, the last note lighter and, with lastShort, let go early); kind \"portato\" = dots under a slur (each note detached a little but carried in one line); kind \"phrase\" = a long phrasing arc (none in this piece). role says what the slur covers.",
  "dyn[]: a printed level at a time, for the voices it was printed for (Busoni prints dynamics per staff, sometimes per voice, e.g. bar 15: p subito in the soprano against mf in the alto's entry).",
  "hairpins[]: cresc/dim spans in time for the given voices. text[]: words printed in the score (quasi stacc., meno legg., poco a poco cresc.) with an English gloss. tempo[]: changes of pace (poco largamente, rall.).",
  "breaths[]: a printed caesura before a note. voicing[]: pianistic re-voicings: octave-doubling (interval in semitones, applied to every note of the voice in the span) and added notes.",
  "rules[]: instructions that apply to unmarked notes (a footnote saying \"always staccato\"); the game must apply these by rule. sections[]: Busoni's double bars.",
  "fingering: a summary and a few samples (fingers in the order of the notes from that note on); not every digit is transcribed.",
  "footnotes[]: German text (as printed, old spelling kept) and an English translation.",
  "conf on every item: sure (clearly printed and placed), likely (printed, the placement or the sign read from a 300 dpi scan with some doubt), unsure (a guess: hard to see, or inferred).",
  "This format is a superset of docs/wtc/readings/*.json: the old per-note subject marks can be derived from marks[] and slurs[] on the first entry."
 ],
